{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c487","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06f12-fa6f-76e2-8836-346b2c16c0e2"},"sequence":158,"recorded_at":1780531400000311,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"442e1aa4-e9ef-4d3f-b619-88e6c26afaec","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"442e1aa4-e9ef-4d3f-b619-88e6c26afaec","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"442e1aa4-e9ef-4d3f-b619-88e6c26afaec"},"call_id":"call_01a06f134d4b73e19dd4ae8d38b3d66d","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-qcosxq/TASK-260830-qcosxq_review-verdict.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-qcosxq review round 1 — verdict: changes requested\n2|\n3|CR `CR-TASK-260830-qcosxq-1` rev 1, base `de51363`, candidate tree\n4|`2c7022bb9fa9c21aab1501a2ed2f1a6b9d26e2bd`, `repository_delta=present`.\n5|Tree verified byte-identical to the candidate before and after the battery\n6|(`git write-tree` == candidate OID).\n7|\n8|## Headline\n9|\n10|| Measure | Leaf 1 (round 4) | This leaf (round 1) |\n11|| --- | ---: | ---: |\n12|| Mutants killed / measured | 80/83 | **44/66** |\n13|| Real survivors | 3 | **18** |\n14|| Resurrections of prior kills | 0 | **0** (13/13 leaf-1 mutants re-killed) |\n15|\n16|44/66 = 67% of the gate rows I derived by traversing production. 22 survived;\n17|4 of those are equivalent (no observable outcome change), leaving **18 gates\n18|that admit what they must reject with the suite green**. Leaf 1's bar was\n19|80/83. This leaf undercuts it.\n20|\n21|The engineering is genuinely good — the refusal-inventory audit, the spec\n22|pinning, the process-group kill, and the two-sided 8 MiB response bound are\n23|all real. The defect is concentrated: three gates are weakenable to values\n24|the pinned spec forbids, and the arm-identity gap that has the sibling Story\n25|in rework is present here at **0 of 23 assertions naming a refusal arm**.\n26|\n27|## Inherited findings — all three confirmed closed\n28|\n29|| Finding | Probe | Result |\n30|| --- | --- | --- |\n31|| F18 seam unpinned | planted a correctly-typed production `init()` reassigning `ownerAttester` | `TestOwnerAttesterIsUnreassigned` **red**; the pin closes round 4's exact mutant |\n32|| F18 residual | weakened `fileOwnerUID` itself (pin cannot see inside) | killed by `TestFileOwnerUIDRefusesWithoutMetadata`, as the closer's comment claims |\n33|| F19 partial set | planted `return out, err` at **all five** refusal returns in `Discover`, not just the two the brief named | 5/5 red |\n34|| F20 Windows invisible | planted a Windows-only type error in `runner_windows.go` | `go build` **rc 0**, `go vet` **rc 0**, full suite **ok** — only `GOOS=windows go vet ./...` fails. The step can fail and fails on its class. |\n35|\n36|F19 is closed more thoroughly than the brief credited. F18's pin is a real\n37|closer, not just present: it fires on the production-`init()` shape that\n38|survived round 4.\n39|\n40|## Findings\n41|\n42|### F21 — `parseMajor`: all four rejection arms misclassify to the wrong Section 15.1 branch, changing the exit code\n43|\n44|`internal/provhost/protocol.go:340-367`. Every one of `parseMajor`'s four\n45|rejection arms can be deleted with the suite green, and three of them flip a\n46|malformed version from `provider_protocol_error` (exit **13**) to\n47|`incompatible_protocol` (exit **6**) — the opposite Section 15.1 branch.\n48|Measured, not inferred:\n49|\n50|| Mutant | `\"3.0\"` | `\".0.0\"` | `\"3.b.c\"` | Suite |\n51|| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n52|| baseline | protocol_error | protocol_error | protocol_error | green |\n53|| M19 delete `len(parts) != 3` | **incompatible** | protocol_error | protocol_error | **green** |\n54|| M20 delete empty-major check | protocol_error | **incompatible** | protocol_error | **green** |\n55|| M22 delete non-digit minor/patch | protocol_error | protocol_error | **incompatible** | **green** |\n56|| M21 delete empty minor/patch | — | — | — | **green** |\n57|\n58|Why it hides: every malformed-version fixture in `TestDecodeResponseRefusals`\n59|has major-part `\"2\"` or a non-numeric first character, so the misclassification\n60|collapses back to the same code. The fixtures prove *malformed → refused*; none\n61|proves *malformed → not a recognizable major*. `doc.go` states \"a\n62|different-major payload is never parsed far enough to trust its error code\" —\n63|the inverse duty, that a non-major is never *promoted* to one, is unpinned.\n64|\n65|Needed: fixtures with a **foreign** major-part in each malformed shape\n66|(`\"3.0\"`, `\"3.0.0.0\"`, `\".0.0\"`, `\"3.b.c\"`, `\"3..0\"`) asserting\n67|`provider_protocol_error`, so each arm's removal reddens.\n68|\n69|### F22 — rollback-token entropy floor weakenable from 256 to 64 bits\n70|\n71|`internal/provhost/status.go:146-149`. The spec floor is 256 bits (32 decoded\n72|bytes). Probed both directions:\n73|\n74|| Bound | Behavior | Suite |\n75|| --- | --- | --- |\n76|| `< 32` (as written) | correct | green |\n77|| `< 16` (128 bits) | accepts a 128-bit token | **green** |\n78|| `< 8` (**64 bits**) | accepts a 64-bit token | **green** |\n79|| `< 48` (384 bits) | rejects the valid fixture | red |\n80|\n81|The gate is provable only in the range (4, 38] bytes: the short fixture\n82|`\"YWJj\"` decodes to **3** bytes and the valid fixture to **38**. Nothing pins\n83|the 32-byte floor itself. A brute-forceable 64-bit rollback token passes this\n84|gate and every test. This is the one survivor with a security consequence —\n85|the token is the only thing standing between the host and a forged rollback\n86|authorization.\n87|\n88|Needed: two-sided boundary fixtures at exactly 31 and 32 decoded bytes.\n89|\n90|### F23 — `readCapped`'s memory bound has zero negative coverage; `doc.go` claims it holds\n91|\n92|`internal/provhost/runner.go:147-156`. Two mutants survive:\n93|\n94|- **R09** — replace `io.ReadAll(io.LimitReader(stream, cap+1))` with\n95|  `io.ReadAll(stream)`: the limiter is gone entirely.\n96|- **R07** — delete the `data[:cap]` truncation.\n97|\n98|Both green. The only `readCapped` test (`TestReadCappedReportsStreamErrors`)\n99|drives the *error* path; nothing drives the *cap* path. `runner.go:145` claims\n100|\"Memory stays bounded no matter what the plugin emits\" and `doc.go:40` states\n101|the 1 MiB stderr cap as a stated bound. Neither reproduces under test — a\n102|capability claim that does not reproduce. A hostile plugin streaming unbounded\n103|stdout/stderr OOMs the host under R09, and no test notices.\n104|\n105|(R08, shrinking `stdoutCap` to 64, *is* killed — but only because it breaks the\n106|positive round-trip. That proves the constant is load-bearing, not that the\n107|cap works.)\n108|\n109|### F24 — request-side 8 MiB bound is one-sided and coarse, while the response side is exact\n110|\n111|`internal/provhost/protocol.go:230`. Asymmetric coverage of the same spec limit:\n112|\n113|| Direction | narrow by 1 byte | widen 2x | halve to 4 MiB |\n114|| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n115|| response (`DecodeResponse:380`) | **killed** | — | — |\n116|| request (`encodeFrame:230`) | **survived** | killed | **survived** |\n117|\n118|`TestDecodeResponseRefusesOversizeFrame` builds exact `limit` and `limit+1`\n119|fixtures for the response. The request fixture is\n120|`strings.Repeat(\"a\", 8<<20)` — roughly 2x over — so the bound is provable only\n121|somewhere between 4 MiB and 16 MiB. Under M06c a spec-legal 8 MiB request is\n122|refused and the suite stays green. The exact-boundary technique already exists\n123|in this file; it was applied to one direction only.\n124|\n125|### F25 — 0 of 23 refusal assertions name the arm; required-member gates slide silently\n126|\n127|The shape the brief flagged, present here. Arm populations: `provider_protocol_error`\n128|has 18 constructor call sites fed by 15 distinct `frameFault` details;\n129|`integrity_failure` has 18 arms. Every one of the 23 `failureCode(t, err)`\n130|assertion sites checks only the stable code — none asserts the `member` or\n131|`detail` that distinguishes the arm.\n132|\n133|Consequence, measured:\n134|\n135|| Mutant | Gate | Suite |\n136|| --- | --- | --- |\n137|| M09 | delete the whole `required` list in `checkResponseMembers` | **green** |\n138|| M09a | narrow it to `{\"ok\"}` | **green** |\n139|| M09b | narrow it to drop `\"ok\"` | **green** |\n140|| S02 | delete the whole 7-member required list in `DecodeStatusOutcome` | **green** |\n141|| S02a | narrow it to `{\"materialization_id\"}` | **green** |\n142|| M27 | delete the value-decode fault in `decodeStrictObject` | **green** |\n143|\n144|Each missing member slides to a lower arm that reports the same code with a\n145|different `member` detail. The gates are currently redundant, so no user-visible\n146|outcome changes today — but they are unverified, and if a lower arm is ever\n147|tightened the missing-member class goes unchecked with nothing to flag it.\n148|\n149|The package's refusal-inventory audit is a real and unusually strong gate — it\n150|killed M24, M28, R04 by itself. But it audits *constructor call sites*, and all\n151|15 `frameFault` arms funnel through single sites, so it is blind to arm-slide by\n152|construction. Asserting the `member`/`detail` on the negative fixtures closes\n153|this cheaply.\n154|\n155|### F26 — `ExecRunner` exit-code plumbing untested; crash classification proven only through the fake\n156|\n157|`internal/provhost/runner.go:134-140`. **R17** — discard `exit.ExitCode()` in\n158|the `*exec.ExitError` branch — survives. Driven through the **production**\n159|runner with a real `exit 3` plugin:\n160|\n161|| | baseline | R17 |\n162|| --- | --- | --- |\n163|| crash-exit plugin via `ExecRunner` | `provider_process_failed` | **`provider_protocol_error`** |\n164|\n165|The empty-stdout classification (`crash → process_failed`, `clean exit →\n166|protocol_error`) is exercised only through `scriptRunner`, which hands `Call` a\n167|pre-set `ExitCode`. The two `ExecRunner` tests cover a clean round-trip and a\n168|timeout; neither exercises a nonzero exit. The classification is proven at the\n169|seam, not at the production entry point.\n170|\n171|### F27 — outbound JSONL line terminator is never asserted\n172|\n173|`internal/provhost/runner.go:97`. **R14** — write `[]byte{}` instead of\n174|`[]byte{'\\n'}` — survives. `TestCallSendsOneFrameToOneProcess` asserts\n175|`call.stdin == frame`, i.e. the frame *without* its terminator; the terminator\n176|is written separately inside `ExecRunner` and observed nowhere. Section 7.2\n177|requires each line to be terminated. I could not demonstrate a failing real\n178|plugin (POSIX `read -r` tolerates an unterminated final line on stdin close),\n179|so I am reporting this as an **uncovered protocol obligation with unproven\n180|impact**, not as a live defect.\n181|\n182|### F28 — README carries a contradictory orphaned paragraph\n183|\n184|`README.md:472-477`. The new `## Provider Plugin JSONL Protocol` section was\n185|spliced in *above* a paragraph that belonged to `## Provider Plugin Discovery\n186|and Trust`. Verified against the base: at `de51363` that paragraph sat under\n187|the provider section. It now reads as a second description of\n188|`internal/provhost` and directly contradicts the paragraph immediately above it:\n189|\n190|> …no Structured Error may be built outside the **six** constructors … the closed **six-code** set.\n191|> The package derives its refusal inventory … outside the **four** constructors … the closed **three-code** set.\n192|\n193|Move it back above the new section.\n194|\n195|### F29 — README/LOGBOOK evidence claim is not representative\n196|\n197|README: \"Each gate is proven by narrowing mutants.\" LOGBOOK 0429: \"25 narrowing\n198|mutants … 25 killed, 0 survivors.\"\n199|\n200|The 25 chosen mutants presumably did all die; I am not disputing that run. But\n201|the claim generalizes to a property that does not reproduce: I narrowed the\n202|rollback-token gate 4x and the request-frame gate 2x, and deleted all four\n203|`parseMajor` arms and the `readCapped` limiter, all green. Either restate the\n204|claim to what was measured, or make it true.\n205|\n206|Credit where due: the LOGBOOK's own ANOMALY entry discloses that an earlier\n207|sweep restored via `git checkout` on untracked files and silently no-opped, so\n208|verdicts were taken on a red mask. That disclosure is exactly right and is why\n209|I re-ran independently rather than accepting the numbers.\n210|\n211|## Producer's reported facts — independently re-verified\n212|\n213|| Claim | Verified |\n214|| --- | ---: |\n215|| `go test ./...` exit 0 (15/15) | yes |\n216|| `-race` clean on `provhost` | yes |\n217|| `gofmt` clean | yes |\n218|| `go vet` and `GOOS=windows go vet` exit 0 | yes |\n219|| `tracecheck` exit 0, `clauses_discharged=17/403`, `acceptance_cases=74` | yes, exact |\n220|| coverage 86.1% | measured **86.3%** — trivial drift, noted only for completeness |\n221|| registry is 15 operations, not 16 | yes — derived from the §7.5 table by the test |\n222|\n223|## Standing bounds — one changed, and not in the direction the brief expected\n224|\n225|- **No production caller of `internal/provider` exists anywhere in the tree.**\n226|  **Unchanged.** `internal/provhost` does *not* import `internal/provider` —\n227|  the only reference is a comment at `runner.go:44`. The brief's expectation\n228|  that this leaf would be the first production caller does not hold.\n229|- **`internal/provhost` itself has no production caller either**, and there is\n230|  no `cmd/ax`. The bound **widens**: two packages now, no entry point. Every\n231|  `Host.Call` assertion is made from a test.\n232|- **Windows behaviour is compile-verified, not executed.** Unchanged, and now\n233|  actually enforced in CI (F20). `runner_windows.go`'s direct-child kill is\n234|  type-checked only; the grandchild-holds-the-pipes case is untested there.\n235|- **The `Inspect`→`ReadFile` TOCTOU window is unprobed.** Unchanged.\n236|- **New bound:** `doc.go`'s non-retryability claim (\"every local failure this\n237|  package emits is non-retryable\") has zero assertions in this package.\n238|\n239|## What this method cannot see\n240|\n241|Mutation rows are derived by traversing production source, so a duty with **no\n242|code at all** — an unimplemented §7.2 clause — leaves no row and is invisible\n243|here. `tracecheck` reports 17/403 clauses discharged; this review measures the\n244|quality of what was built, not the size of what was not. Concurrency\n245|interleavings beyond `-race` on the existing tests are also outside the method,\n246|as is real Windows execution.\n247|\n248|## Verdict\n249|\n250|**changes requested → `to-dev`.**\n251|\n252|Blocking: **F21**, **F22**, **F23** — three gates admit what the pinned spec\n253|forbids, with a wrong exit code, a 4x-weakened entropy floor, and an unproven\n254|resource bound respectively. **F24**, **F26**, **F28** should land in the same\n255|round; they are small. **F25** is the round's structural ask: assert the\n256|`member`/`detail` on the existing negative fixtures so refusal arms stop\n257|sliding. **F27** and **F29** are honesty/coverage items, not defects.\n258|\n259|Re-run bar for round 2: every survivor above killed, no resurrection among the\n260|13 leaf-1 mutants re-verified here, and the report stated as a killed/measured\n261|ratio over a production-derived traversal.\n262|\n263|## Harness\n264|\n265|`.temp/TASK-260830-qcosxq/mut/` — `m.sh` (apply / full-package run / restore),\n266|`apply.py` (fails closed unless the pattern matches exactly once), `restore.sh`\n267|(`cp` from a pristine backup, never `git checkout`), `logs/` (one per mutant).\n268|Every run is full-package: `inventory_test.go`'s audit only fires when\n269|`-run` is empty, and three kills came from it alone.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
